+Review complete. Scripts-only change (city import pipeline + static render); no NATS subject/payload or network-format changes, so no ADR trigger. No determinism risk to the engine — all randomness/wall-clock stays in offline tooling (`time.time()` in render-city.py is logging only), and script outputs are deterministic (insertion-ordered dicts, `sorted()` at emission points). Verified against repo: netimport flags (`-in/-out/-name/-bbox/-report`) all exist; generated scenario.yaml matches `engine/scenario/scenario.go:63-88`; lane fields `shape/internal/row/tl/speedLimit` match `engine/netfile.go:50-79`.
+
+## Blockers
+
+None.
+
+## Should-fix
+
+1. **overpass2osm.py:349-354 — "real nodes win over synthesis" is element-order-dependent.** A mixed-shape way (both `nodes` and `geometry`) that appears *before* its node elements synthesizes coords (`ref not in real_nodes` is vacuously true), and the real node is later emitted too → duplicate `<node id=…>` in the XML (synth emission at line 370 never re-checks `real_nodes`). The docstring's mixed-shape guarantee only holds when nodes precede ways. Filter `synth_nodes` against `real_nodes` at emission.
+
+2. **osm-stop-nodes.py:145-146 — way-split nodes count as junctions.** OSM routinely splits one road into consecutive ways; the shared end node has ≥2 distinct way ids but is not an intersection. Consequences: (a) a stop node sitting at a split point takes the `holding >= 2` branch (line 165-167) → spurious `allway_stop` at a 1-in/1-out through node; (b) walks terminate at the first split point (line 180-183), retyping a node upstream of the real intersection — stop control lands mid-road. Doesn't break netconvert (every shared node is a junction in the no-join net), but misplaces control.
+
+3. **osm-stop-nodes.py:191 — arm aggregation conflates a through way's two approaches into one arm.** T-junction: through way A, ending way B. Stops on one A-approach and on B give `arms == {A, B} == incident` → `allway_stop`, though the opposite A-approach is uncontrolled. A per-direction arm key (way id + walk sign) would fix it.
+
+## Nits
+
+- **import-city.sh:26** — `$NC` hardcodes `python3.12` in the venv path and is never existence-checked; failure mode is a cryptic "command not found" mid-run. (Matches the contract doc's recipe verbatim, so acceptable, but a `[ -x "$NC" ]` guard is one line.)
+- **render-city.py:440** — comment "paper.casing #e5e5e8 is invisible" is stale: working-tree `theme.ts` paper.casing is already `#9a9aa1`, identical to the render value — it's no longer an adaptation. Also note the paper theme / `stopFace` / `hudText` fields this file cites as "source of truth" exist only in the **unstaged** `viz/src/theme.ts` diff, not at HEAD — if this commit lands first, its pointers dangle until the viz commit follows. Commit-ordering awareness, not a code change.
+- **scripts/__pycache__/** is generated by the tests (untracked now) and `.gitignore` has no `__pycache__` entry.
+- **osm-stop-nodes.py:151, overpass2osm.py types** — `int | None` / `dict[int, str]` annotations require Python ≥3.10; scripts invoke bare `python3`. Fine per the repo's 3.12 assumption; will confuse on older boxes.
+- **osm-stop-nodes.py:174** — `refs.index(nid)` walks only the first occurrence of the stop node in closed/looped ways.
+
+## Questions
+
+- **import-city.sh:55-60** — netimport's `-source` provenance flag is skipped; intentional that these city imports carry no provenance stamp?
+- **contracts/network-format-v1.md:162** — the bootstrap recipe predates the two-pass stop workflow and JSON input this script implements. Per ground rule 2 ("if your work contradicts the KB, the KB is stale — fix it"), is a doc update queued? (Docs-only commit passes the gate ungated.)
+
+REVIEW-COMPLETE
