@@ -0,0 +1,20 @@
+**No blockers.** Determinism and contract discipline check out: Python outputs are sorted or insertion-ordered from input (the one set `.pop()` at osm-stop-nodes.py:222 is provably single-element since `is_split` guarantees exactly 2 ways), wall clock is excluded from hashed provenance (import-city.sh refuses a missing extract timestamp; the Go `time.Now` fallback is documented and bypassed by the canned pipeline), the provenance semantic widening is recorded in both the contract doc and ADR-0017, and no NATS subjects/payloads change. The scenario.yaml emitted by import-city.sh matches the `engine/scenario` schema (`types: []string`, `spawner.rate_per_lane_h`), and the `rowClass` claim in the stop-override docstring matches `engine/netimport/netimport.go:553-563`.
+
+**Should-fix**
+
+1. `scripts/osm-stop-nodes.py:238` (aggregation `len(j["arms"]) >= total`) — against-flow arms can be marked but never counted, inflating toward spurious `allway_stop`. The comment at :169-171 claims the `travel` filter rejects against-flow arms, but it only fires when *every* way at the stop node is oneway. Two escapes: (a) a stop on a mixed split node (two-way way + oneway continuation, same name/class) with `direction=backward` walks the oneway against flow and marks a junction where `incident_arms` credits that way 0 arms; (b) a split hop (:217-232) can land on a oneway continuation and keep walking against its flow — hop direction is chosen by node order only, oneway is never consulted. Either mismarks a junction the sign doesn't control (`priority_stop` at minimum, `allway_stop` if the legit arms are all stopped). Fix is cheap: check `oneway_tag(cur)` against the walk sign before `mark()`, and on hops.
+
+**Nits**
+
+2. `scripts/osm-stop-nodes.py:178-181` — an explicit `direction` that fights the oneway flow drops the stop silently (empty `steps`), unlike the `defaulted` counter which reports. One more stderr counter keeps mapper errors visible; ADR-0017 §4 says "dropped" so behavior itself is per spec.
+3. `scripts/osm-stop-nodes.py:194` — a directionless stop sitting *on* a split node walks forward on **both** ways with the same sign; relative to the road these are opposite directions, so one physical sign can mark junctions on both sides. Over-marking, mild.
+4. `scripts/osm-stop-nodes.py:184-193` — OSM `direction` also occurs as compass values (`NE`, degrees) on stop nodes; those fall into the defaulted-forward branch. Reported, so acceptable — noting it in the docstring would prevent surprise.
+
+**Questions**
+
+5. `scripts/import-city.sh:49-53` — does the pass-2 `--sumo-net-file` reprocessing recompute connections/priorities at junctions *other than* the re-typed ones (roundabouts, ramp priorities)? If a pass1-vs-pass2 net diff on one of the six metros was checked, a one-line note in ADR-0017 closes this; if not, worth one look before recordings bind the geometry.
+6. ADR-0017 §3 deviates from ADR-0009's turn-restriction intent — should ADR-0009 get a cross-reference annotation so a reader starting there finds the deviation? (adrs.md rows exist; the ADR file itself is the question.)
+
+Finding 1 is the only one I'd act on before commit, and even it is defensible under the triage bar as an accepted approximation — but the ADR/comment currently *claims* the guard exists, so either add the oneway check or soften the claim at osm-stop-nodes.py:169-171 and ADR-0017 §4.
+
+REVIEW-COMPLETE
